{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "catalog": "evidscope-threat-scenarios-contrast",
  "version": 3,
  "notes": "Contrast scenarios (adversarial audit 2026-10-17, fix 3): the same rule alert with a different designed answer depending on context (read vs edit of the audit settings, reading a secret file vs reading and sending it, force-push to an own branch vs main, a chain inside vs outside the sequence window), plus ordinary development sessions. Threat steps are references into catalog.json ({from, step}), so no threat command is repeated here; {set, count, offset} expands a named run of ordinary development steps. Labels are designer labels fixed at creation, like catalog.json. Version 2 adds read-versus-edit pairs for the other audit-relevant settings files (Codex hooks, Codex settings, EvidScope collector settings): reading them is ordinary, editing them is tampering with what records the agent. The Claude Code settings pair stays out of training as the held-out test of whether the model learned the principle rather than the file name. Version 3 (judge round 2, 2026-10-17) adds the same read-versus-change principle in more shapes: single-step sessions (a lone read is ordinary, a lone edit is tampering), shell and PowerShell reads and writes, and the EvidScope hook script. The Claude Code settings files still appear only in held-out families.",
